## 13. Security Testing Recommendations

| Test case | Expected behavior |
|---|---|
| Intended recipient accepts invitation | Accepted |
| Different authenticated email accepts invitation | HTTP 403 |
| User from another organization accepts invitation | Rejected unless explicitly authorized |
| Unauthenticated user accepts invitation | HTTP 401 |
| Expired invitation is accepted | Rejected |
| Already accepted invitation is redeemed again | Rejected |
| Revoked invitation is accepted | Rejected |
| Invitation token is modified | Rejected |
| User attempts acceptance with an unverified email | Rejected |




## 14. Conclusion

The assessment identified an invitation acceptance authorization failure in the application's API.

An authenticated account was able to accept an invitation intended for a different email address and associated with another organization's project.

The acceptance endpoint returned a successful HTTP response, and subsequent retrieval of the invitation confirmed that its status had changed from `pending` to `accepted`.

The vulnerability demonstrates the importance of enforcing recipient email ownership validation and organization-level authorization within invitation acceptance workflows.

The application should ensure that only the intended, verified recipient can redeem an invitation and obtain its associated membership role.

